Sval: the accessibility checks accept a treeitem reached by a roving tabindex

`isKeyboardFocusable` takes an element with a negative tabindex as one a
keyboard user cannot reach, apart from the roving-tabindex widgets it
names: toolbars, moz-radio and moz-visual-picker. WAI-ARIA's tree view
pattern keeps one treeitem in the tab order and moves among the rest with
the arrow keys, so a click on any other treeitem of a valid tree (Sval's
panel rows) failed as "Node is not focusable via the accessibility API".
A treeitem whose tree has a treeitem at tabindex 0 is now accepted.

diff --git a/testing/mochitest/tests/SimpleTest/AccessibilityUtils.js b/testing/mochitest/tests/SimpleTest/AccessibilityUtils.js
index 0774e48e0c..96f0ff31e8 100644
--- a/testing/mochitest/tests/SimpleTest/AccessibilityUtils.js
+++ b/testing/mochitest/tests/SimpleTest/AccessibilityUtils.js
@@ -789,6 +789,12 @@ this.AccessibilityUtils = (function () {
           node.getRootNode().host?.localName === "moz-radio") ||
         (role === Ci.nsIAccessibleRole.ROLE_RADIOBUTTON &&
           node.getRootNode().host?.localName === "moz-visual-picker-item") ||
+        // An ARIA tree keeps one treeitem in the tab order and reaches the
+        // rest with the arrow keys (https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/treeview/).
+        (role === Ci.nsIAccessibleRole.ROLE_OUTLINEITEM &&
+          node
+            .closest('[role="tree"]')
+            ?.querySelector('[role="treeitem"][tabindex="0"]')) ||
         shouldIgnoreTabIndex(node))
     );
   }
